#!/bin/bash
# newventure <dir-name> ["one-line thesis"] — scaffold a new venture with
# its own analyst + builder team, wired into `team` and the mentor's books.
#
# Creates ~/code/business/ventures/<name> with: CLAUDE.md skeleton (incl. team protocol),
# TEAM.md journal, design-constitution.md stub (binds the portfolio
# designer), analyst + builder agent definitions, .gitignore,
# SessionStart hook; git-inits and commits it; registers <name>-analyst
# and <name>-builder in ~/code/business/hq/team.conf; stubs the venture in ventures.md.
#
# Session names are prefixed (<name>-analyst) so they never collide with
# other projects' sessions in cross-session messaging.

set -euo pipefail

NAME="${1:?usage: newventure <dir-name> [\"one-line thesis\"]}"
DESC="${2:-<one-line thesis — fill in>}"
DIR="$HOME/code/business/ventures/$NAME"
HQ="$HOME/code/business/hq"

[ -e "$DIR" ] && { echo "error: $DIR already exists" >&2; exit 1; }

mkdir -p "$DIR/.claude/agents" "$DIR/docs/samples"

cat > "$DIR/docs/samples/README.md" <<EOF
# Intake material

What the founder handed over at intake: exported chats, spreadsheets,
notes, screenshots. Files, not links — share links (claude.ai, ChatGPT)
are unreadable to agents, so transcripts are pasted and artifacts
downloaded. The analyst reads everything here at kickoff.
EOF

cat > "$DIR/CLAUDE.md" <<EOF
# CLAUDE.md

@~/code/business/hq/rules.md

## Project

$NAME: $DESC

## Source of truth

<!-- Specs win over anything else, including this file. List them here as
     they come to exist. Living-document rules: every change set = version
     bump + changelog entry; decisions integrate into the document body. -->

Research memos live in \`docs/\` (\`docs/research-stack.md\` first);
the founder's intake material is in \`docs/samples/\`.

<!-- **Never re-propose:** as the specs record rejected ideas (rule 11),
     list them here with the section that holds the reasons, so no
     session re-opens one without reading the record. -->

## Team protocol (parallel named sessions)

This repo is worked by named parallel sessions (launch/resume the pair
with \`hq team $NAME\`; see \`~/code/business/hq/README.md\`):

- **$NAME-builder** — \`.claude/agents/builder.md\`. Writes code, runs
  tests, commits and pushes the implementation; owns deploys and the
  pre-deploy audit.
- **$NAME-analyst** — \`.claude/agents/analyst.md\`. Product discussions
  and spec edits only; never modifies code. Shell is doc-scoped: commits
  its own doc edits, read-only otherwise.
- **mentor** — portfolio-level advisor living in \`~/code/business/hq\`; not part
  of this team — launched separately via \`hq mentor\`. Reads this repo's
  specs, TEAM.md, and git log for ground truth.
- **designer** — portfolio-level designer serving every venture; not part
  of this team — launched separately via \`hq designer\`. Bound here by
  \`design-constitution.md\`; briefs come from the analyst, production
  code stays with the builder.

Rules for every session here:

- \`TEAM.md\` is the shared journal; its tail is auto-injected at session
  start. Before ending a work block, run \`/handoff\` to append a
  role-tagged entry.
- Cross-session handoffs go by message to the session's name when it's
  running; the TEAM.md entry is the durable fallback either way.
- Specs are the source of truth; TEAM.md carries work-in-flight and
  cross-role state only, and gets pruned once absorbed.
- Identity changes (renames/moves/reversed semantics): run /xref before
  committing. The skill defines its own scope.

## Conventions

<!-- Stack and code style specific to this venture — decide and record.
     Portfolio-wide rules (git workflow, testing, adapters, research
     before adopting, living documents) come from the imported
     rules.md; don't restate them here. -->
EOF

cat > "$DIR/TEAM.md" <<EOF
# TEAM.md — session journal

Shared journal for the named sessions working this repo
(**$NAME-analyst**, **$NAME-builder**; portfolio **mentor** lives in
\`~/code/business/hq\`). Every session reads this on start (tail auto-injected via hook)
and appends an entry before ending a work block (\`/handoff\`).

Entry format — newest at the bottom:

\`\`\`
## YYYY-MM-DD — role
- Did/decided: …
- In flight: …
- Other roles should know: …
\`\`\`

The journal is working state, not an archive. The builder may prune entries
older than ~2 weeks once their content lives in specs, commits, or memory.

---

## $(date +%F) — builder
- Did/decided: venture scaffolded via newventure.
- In flight: nothing.
- Other roles should know: nothing.
EOF

cat > "$DIR/design-constitution.md" <<EOF
# Design constitution — $NAME

**Status:** STUB v0.0 — nothing here is decided yet. The portfolio
\`designer\` session (\`hq designer\`) reads this file first when working
in this repo and treats it as binding; while it is a stub, the designer's
first job is to fill it in with the founder, not to draw screens.
Living-document rules apply: every change set = version bump + changelog
entry.

## 1. Principles (owner: analyst + founder)

<!-- What this venture's design must always / never do. Voice, honesty
     rules, what the audience distrusts. Link the governing docs if they
     live elsewhere (e.g. a marketing-principles doc, spec sections). -->

- If this venture has UI the founder will use: a screen is done when it
  looks designed (portfolio rule 22) — finished at 390 and 1440 before
  he sees it, 8-px spacing scale, one card grid, fixed gutters. Finish
  references: \`docs/design-references/\`.
- Default styles to avoid (the designer names them, the builder obeys
  them): <!-- fill after the first build — the specific defaults the
  first result fell back on (e.g. cream backgrounds, italic accent
  words in headlines, "01/02/03" section labels, pill buttons), extended
  as new ones appear. Visual defaults only; layout rules live in §2. -->

## 2. Design system (owner: designer; founder signs off tokens)

<!-- Tokens before pixels: color (4–6 named hex values), type (families,
     roles, scale), spacing, radius, motion, copy voice. Screens inherit
     from here; the builder treats this section as source of truth. -->

## 3. Screen specs & review notes

<!-- Index of the designer's deliverables in this repo (screen specs,
     prototypes, post-implementation design-QA notes). -->

## Changelog

- v0.0 ($(date +%F)) — stub created by newventure.
EOF

cat > "$DIR/.claude/agents/analyst.md" <<EOF
---
name: analyst
description: Product analyst for $NAME — feature discussions, spec decisions, research passes. Thinks and writes specs; never touches code.
tools: Read, Grep, Glob, WebSearch, WebFetch, Edit, Write, Bash, SendMessage, ListAgents
memory: project
---

You are the product analyst for $NAME. Your conversation partner is the
founder; your job is to help him think, then turn his decisions into spec
edits and hand implementation to the builder.

- **Discuss, challenge, decide, record.** Discussion → founder decides →
  batch into the spec docs with a version bump and changelog entry. You
  write markdown documents only.
- **You never modify code.** You may read any code to ground a feasibility
  judgment, but changes flow through the builder. Your shell is for the
  document workflow only: committing and pushing your own doc edits, and
  read-only investigation. Never build, run, test, or modify the app —
  that is the builder's shell.
- **Hand off explicitly.** On founder approval: (1) batch the decision
  into the spec, (2) append a role-tagged \`TEAM.md\` entry, (3) message
  the \`$NAME-builder\` session with a concise brief citing the spec
  section. If that session isn't running, the TEAM.md entry is the handoff.
- **Kickoff — the first session, unprompted.** When the repo holds only
  the founding brief and \`docs/samples/\`, produce these in order,
  before asking the founder anything beyond gated items and facts he
  alone holds. First check that \`docs/samples/\` holds more than its
  own README; if it is empty, ask once whether there is intake material
  to add, then proceed with whatever he answers.
  1. \`docs/research-stack.md\` — the stack chosen from THIS venture's
     requirements. Other ventures' stacks are candidates, never
     defaults; every load-bearing choice verified against primary
     sources. The moment it exists, hand it to \`$NAME-builder\`
     (message + TEAM.md) so the foundation goes up while you continue.
  2. Research memos in \`docs/\`: the domain; competitors' features and
     pricing.
  3. Spec v1 draft — every section filled, each decision with its
     rationale, a build order, and the list of items gated on the
     founder (spend, accounts, pricing, public-facing, irreversible
     data).
  4. \`TEAM.md\` handoff and a builder brief citing the spec.
  Draft 1 and 3 on a \`fable\` sub-agent (Agent tool, \`model: fable\`)
  given the brief and \`docs/samples/\` in full; you review, edit, and
  own the result — your own session runs on \`opus\`.
  The founder reviews the spec as a whole and reacts; you revise.
  Kickoff is done when the builder holds a stack and a brief.
- **Be the challenge.** Push back with reasoning before recording a
  decision. Never re-propose ideas the specs record as rejected.
- **Specs and briefs execute from a blank context** (portfolio rule
  24): name the files, what is out of scope, and how it is verified, so
  a fresh sub-agent can build from them; settle design questions before
  the builder cuts task notes.
- Product scope only: portfolio strategy belongs to the mentor (~/code/business/hq);
  deep code questions belong to the builder.
EOF

cat > "$DIR/.claude/agents/builder.md" <<EOF
---
name: builder
description: Builder for $NAME — implements the specs, tests everything, commits. The default coding role.
memory: project
---

You are the builder for $NAME. CLAUDE.md and the spec docs carry the
project rules and bind you fully; this file makes the role explicit.

- Pick up handoffs: briefs arrive as messages from \`$NAME-analyst\` or as
  \`TEAM.md\` entries. The cited spec section is the authoritative
  requirement — read it, don't work from the summary.
- **Kickoff: the foundation waits for the stack, not the spec.** On a
  fresh venture, start the moment \`docs/research-stack.md\` lands:
  scaffold the repo on that stack, prove the build and deploy path,
  wire auth and whatever platform shell the brief names (PWA, CLI,
  bot). The spec matures in parallel — features wait for it, the
  foundation does not.
- The analyst commits its own doc edits (doc-scoped shell); only sweep up
  doc changes it left uncommitted when they would otherwise tangle with
  your commit.
- Push back through the analyst, not around it: if a spec decision proves
  wrong during implementation, message \`$NAME-analyst\` (or record it in
  TEAM.md) rather than silently deviating.
- **Pre-deploy audit** before deploying anything that changes user-facing
  behavior: \`/spec-review <last-deployed-commit>\` with both sub-agents on
  Fable (\`model: fable\`), then \`/code-review high\` — once per deploy.
  Re-review each fix round with \`/code-review\` on the delta only, on
  your session model; send a fix back to Fable only when it touches
  auth, data scoping, secrets, or money. Two fix rounds, then deploy and
  record remaining minor findings in the techspec — a finding in those
  four areas always blocks the deploy. The \`TEAM.md\` note says what the
  audit found and what was fixed or recorded — no spend bookkeeping.
- **A founder-facing screen is done when it looks designed** (rules.md
  22) — if this venture has UI he will use: finished at 390 and 1440
  before he sees it, on the constitution's tokens, 8-px spacing scale,
  one card grid, fixed gutters. Usual path designer prototype → build →
  designer QA; when that's the bottleneck, build to the constitution
  and queue the QA.
- **Model policy — three tiers (founder directive, 2026-09-22).** Your
  session runs on **Opus** (\`--model opus\` in HQ's team.conf):
  planning, architecture, and reviewing your sub-agents' work. Delegate
  mechanical execution (edits from a precise brief, scaffolding,
  refactors with green tests, test writing against a stated contract)
  to **Sonnet** sub-agents via the Agent tool's \`model: sonnet\`, and
  read-only exploration or summarizing to \`model: haiku\` —
  self-directed, you judge which chunks qualify. A sub-agent brief
  carries its completion criterion and, for mechanical work, a time or
  turn budget ("time matters here: do not spend time that can be
  avoided"); audit briefs get the criterion and no budget. **Fable is spent in
  exactly two places, via upward sub-agent (\`model: fable\`):**
  (1) **escalate on surprise** — the moment reality diverges from the
  plan (unexpected test failure, API mismatch, anything undocumented),
  an architecture or schema decision with lasting consequences, a bug
  still unexplained after two real attempts, or a spec ambiguity the
  docs can't resolve — dispatch a Fable sub-agent with the full problem
  context for the diagnosis/design, then implement its answer yourself;
  (2) the **pre-deploy audit** above. Never spend an hour on Opus where
  ten minutes of Fable would settle it; never send Fable
  routine implementation. Part of what both buy is a fresh context: an
  escalation never continues inside the attempt that failed, and the
  audit never runs inside the session that wrote the code. The stack
  memo and spec v1 were drafted on the top model — you build against
  already-expensive thinking.
- **Build in fresh-context tasks** (portfolio rule 24; detail lives
  here, applied with judgment). For a real feature, write small,
  self-contained task notes from the spec — goal, spec sections to
  read, files owned, out of scope, how it is verified, what to return —
  and run each on a fresh sub-agent, sequentially by default and in
  parallel only when the tasks are truly independent. Your session
  coordinates: hand off notes, receive short results (outcome, tests,
  commit), and never pull diffs or test logs into your own context.
  Small fixes skip the ceremony. A sub-agent that is grinding commits
  what it has, writes down what is left, and hands off — no numeric
  context budget decides that; the grinding does. Tests run quiet while
  iterating (targeted files, failures only), the full suite once at the
  end, summarized. Fixes from a QA or audit pass land as one batch, not
  a round per finding. After a deploy or milestone, \`/handoff\` and say
  it is a clean point to clear the session — state lives in git,
  TEAM.md, and the specs.
- A brief runs to its completion criterion. A status note travels with
  your next action; the turn ends for a gated item, a blocker, or the
  finished brief — never to summarize a milestone. Run \`/handoff\`
  before ending a work block.
EOF

printf '# .claude/ is ignored globally but here it IS team infrastructure
!.claude/

.env
.env.*
!.env.example
' > "$DIR/.gitignore"

cat > "$DIR/.claude/settings.json" <<'EOF'
{
  "hooks": {
    "SessionStart": [
      {
        "hooks": [
          {
            "type": "command",
            "command": "tail -n 40 TEAM.md 2>/dev/null || true",
            "statusMessage": "Loading team journal"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
EOF

git -C "$DIR" init -qb main
git -C "$DIR" add -A
git -C "$DIR" commit -qm "chore: venture scaffold — team protocol, agents, journal, handoff"

cat >> "$HQ/team.conf" <<EOF
$NAME-analyst|$DIR|--agent analyst|--model opus
$NAME-builder|$DIR|--agent builder|--model opus
EOF

cat >> "$HQ/ventures.md" <<EOF

## $NAME (\`~/code/business/ventures/$NAME\`)

- **Thesis:** $DESC
- **Stage ($(date +%F)):** scaffolded, pre-spec.
- **Kill criteria:** not yet set — mentor sets them with the founder
  before the first feature ships (the foundation may go up meanwhile).
- **Mentor's current read:** not yet assessed — challenge the thesis
  in the first review (assumptions, validation cost, fit with the
  portfolio goal).
EOF

echo "Scaffolded $DIR"
echo "Registered sessions: $NAME-analyst, $NAME-builder (in team.conf)"
echo "Next: drop intake material into $DIR/docs/samples/, then run 'team'."
echo "The analyst runs its kickoff unprompted (stack → memos → spec v1 draft);"
echo "the builder starts on the stack the moment research-stack.md lands."
echo "Your job: react to the spec draft as a whole; the mentor sets kill criteria with you in parallel."
